---
// Manifesto: site.md §2, copy verbatim, laid out as the wall (site.css). One principle to a screen: its number a meta
// label at the hairline, its first sentence a statement at Display, the rest a caption in the caption half under it.
// The title screen and the closing are statements of their own; the closing carries the stamp and the button.
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
import Stamp from "../components/Stamp.astro";
---

<Site passage title="Manifesto" description="Five principles I work by." image="/manifesto-og.png">
  <main class="wall manifesto">
    <section class="intro grid">
      <h1 class="label">MANIFESTO</h1>
      <p class="statement"><span>Five principles</span> <span>I&nbsp;work&nbsp;by.</span></p>
    </section>

    <article class="part principle grid">
      <span class="label num">01.</span>
      <h2 class="statement"><span>Every decision</span> <span>has an <em>author</em>.</span></h2>
      <div class="cap">
        <p>If no one will defend it, it shouldn't ship. Every product is built on a few thousand small decisions: what the cancel button says, where the password strength meter sits, whether the empty state is funny or quiet. Most of those get made by accident. I make them on&nbsp;purpose.</p>
        <p>Settings are unfinished decisions. So is sending people to support for something the screen could have told them. "Make it customizable" is what designers say when they can't&nbsp;decide.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="part principle grid">
      <span class="label num">02.</span>
      <h2 class="statement"><span class="ph"><span>The brief is wrong</span> <span>until proven&nbsp;right.</span></span></h2>
      <div class="cap">
        <p>A brief is the founder's first guess at the problem. The work starts with checking that guess. If you hand me a brief and I do exactly what it says, I'm not designing. I'm&nbsp;typing.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="part principle grid">
      <span class="label num">03.</span>
      <h2 class="statement"><span>Make the call,</span> <span>then check&nbsp;it.</span></h2>
      <div class="cap">
        <p>Research sharpens a decision. It doesn't replace one. I decide from what I've seen, and research where I'm&nbsp;unsure.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="part principle grid">
      <span class="label num">04.</span>
      <h2 class="statement"><span>Character is</span> <span>not a&nbsp;layer.</span></h2>
      <div class="cap">
        <p>A product has one voice or none. The voice shows up in places people don't think of as design: the wording on a 404, the timing of a toast notification, what your invoice email feels like. If those don't match the rest of the product, the product doesn't have a voice. It has a&nbsp;styleguide.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="part principle grid">
      <span class="label num">05.</span>
      <h2 class="statement"><span>The safe version</span> <span>is already&nbsp;taken.</span></h2>
      <div class="cap">
        <p>Every category has its safe version, and it's already taken. The banking app in navy blue. The pharmacy site that opens with a sale. Building another one puts you at the back of a line someone else started. You should be able to tell whose product it is without seeing the&nbsp;logo.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- The closing: a statement like the principles, the stamp's rim crossing "build —", the button as its caption. -->
    <section class="part end grid">
      <Stamp seed={1} prefer={0} />
      <p class="statement" data-stamp-host><span>If this sounds like</span> <span>how you want</span> <span>to <span data-stamp-at>build&nbsp;—</span></span></p>
      <div class="cap">
        <a class="btn" href="/review/" data-gem>Send a link, get a video review <span class="arw">→</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Site>

<style>
  /* The title screen: the label, the statement, and the rest of the first screen empty, with the first principle's
     hairline and number just showing at its foot. */
  .intro {
    min-height: calc(100svh - 150px);
    align-content: start;
  }
  .intro .label,
  .intro .statement,
  .num {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
  }
  .intro .statement,
  .part > .statement {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin-top: 72px;
  }
  .part > .cap {
    margin-top: 48px;
  }

  /* A principle fills a screen: its hairline at the top of the window, the next one just under the fold. */
  .principle {
    min-height: 100svh;
    align-content: start;
  }
  .cap {
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
  }
  .end {
    padding-bottom: 96px;
    align-content: start;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .intro {
      min-height: calc(100svh - 120px);
    }
    .intro .statement,
    .part > .statement {
      margin-top: 48px;
    }
    .part > .cap {
      margin-top: 32px;
    }
    /* A phone keeps a screen to a principle too: the rest of the screen is the pause before the next one. */
    .principle {
      padding-bottom: 48px;
    }
    .end {
      padding-bottom: 64px;
    }
  }
</style>
